<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Curaçao</h1><p class="st-meta">2010 CE – present · capital Willemstad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Island off Venezuela, the former centre of the Dutch Caribbean, which became a separate country within the Kingdom of the Netherlands in 2010.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/netherlands-antilles">Netherlands Antilles</a> were dissolved on 10 October 2010, Curaçao became an autonomous country within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands</a>, after a referendum in 2005 had favoured separate status. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerrit-schotte">Gerrit Schotte</a> became its first prime minister, governing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willemstad">Willemstad</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Curaçao has its own parliament, constitution and government, while the kingdom handles defence and foreign affairs, and a shared central bank issues the guilder with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sint-maarten">Sint Maarten</a>. The economy rests on tourism, the port, offshore finance and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isla-refinery">Isla oil refinery</a>, which was operated by Venezuela's state oil company until 2019 and has stood idle since. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papiamento">Papiamentu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English are official languages. The murder of politician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmin-wiels">Helmin Wiels</a> in 2013 shook public life.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Curaçao remains a country within the kingdom, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilmar-pisas">Gilmar Pisas</a> as prime minister since 2021. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willemstad">Willemstad's</a> colourful colonial harbour is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, and the crisis in neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela</a> has strained its economy and borders.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/curacao.jpg" alt="Curaçao" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: David Benbennick · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cura%C3%A7ao.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2010 CE – present</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Willemstad</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gerrit Schotte, Gilmar Pisas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Papiamentu, Dutch and English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/curacao">Curaçao</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Country of Curaçao</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2018&amp;focus=curacao" aria-label="Curaçao on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2786 1726 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Curaçao lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3106 1982L5110 626M3106 1982L3106 1982"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5110 626h0M3106 1982h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3106 1982h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3106 1982h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Willemstad. <a href="/atlas?year=2018&amp;focus=curacao">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
